      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To observe and describe the positions and movements women choose while immersed in water during the first stage of labor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Descriptive, observational pilot study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "A rural community hospital that provided hydrotherapy in labor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "Women (N = 7) who intended to use hydrotherapy in labor were recruited prenatally from a midwife-managed practice."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASURES",
          "content": "For 15 minutes of each hour during the first stage of labor, position and movements of the participants were observed and recorded on a laptop computer. The observational tool was developed for this study from a review of the literature and interviews with nursing experts; 435 observations were recorded. Women were free to choose when and how long to use hydrotherapy and had no restriction on their positions and movements."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Only 3 of the 7 participants labored in the tub. Women demonstrated a greater range of positions and movements in the tub than in bed, both throughout labor and during late first-stage labor (7-10 cm of dilatation). Women had more contractions and made more rhythmic movements while in the tub than in bed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Hydrotherapy may encourage upright positions and movements that facilitate labor progress and coping, helping women avoid unnecessary interventions."
        }
      ]},
